graph examples {
  meta: { description: "Growth architecture guide", type: "core", output: "_promptExamples" }

  let prompt = set(key: "_promptExamples", value: "## Growth Architecture Guide\n\nA growth is an application unit in the AITOS system. Each button has its own addEventListener that directly calls its handler. No router layer needed.\n\n### Behavior Rules\n\nWhen creating or modifying a growth, follow these rules strictly:\n\n1. **Self-check before saving** - Before calling save-growth(), verify every atom name exists, every parameter name matches, and no if is nested more than 2 levels.\n2. **One-shot success as the goal** - Aim to save a correct graph on the first attempt. Do NOT repeatedly call save-growth() in a trial-and-error loop.\n3. **Report failures honestly** - If save-growth() returns { success: false, error: \"...\" }, report the real error and fix it. NEVER pretend a failed save succeeded.\n4. **Every response must progress** - Each response should either (a) make progress with a tool call, or (b) deliver the final result to the user. Do not end with only a plan or a promise of future action.\n5. **Inspect before modifying** - Use get-growth-code(name) to view existing source before modifying a growth. Use get-growth-log(name) and analyze-telemetry() to learn from past mistakes instead of repeating them.\n6. **Batch independent tool calls** - When you need several pieces of information that do not depend on each other (e.g. list-growths + getSkillSet + get-growth-code), request them together in a single response instead of one tool call per turn. Batching avoids resending the whole conversation on every extra round-trip.\n\n### Explicit Data Flow\n\nCore principle: AI must know the structure of any data it uses WITHOUT reading atom source code and WITHOUT guessing. The standard differs by the data's source:\n\n1. **Event data** (runtime, high-frequency, transient) - Use explicit keys. The key name IS the field name, e.g. inputKeydown_key / inputKeydown_shiftKey / appClick_targetId. Do NOT read the event object and getProp its fields.\n2. **Atom return values** (API responses, file picker, tool results) - The atom's meta.output must declare the FULL structure, including nested fields. AI sees it via getSkillSet(), so getProp is legitimate once the structure is declared. Example: httpStreamRequest output = { role, content, toolCalls: [{ id, type, function: { name, arguments } }] }.\n3. **File / persisted data** (conversations, models, growth index) - The structure IS the file itself. AI opens the file and sees it, e.g. readLocal(\"conversations\") returns [{ id, title }], so getProp(conv, \"id\") is legitimate. Do not split.\n\n**Bottom line (the only failure case):** A box created inside atom code, not declared in meta, with no file backing, whose structure is only knowable from source code. This kind of data must be eliminated - either by splitting explicit keys (event class) or declaring the structure in meta (return-value class).\n\nAtom internal code is never read by AI - write it freely.\n\n### Directory Structure\n\ngrowth-name/\n  main.acs          Main graph: init state, build UI, bind events per button\n  handle-xxx.acs    Sub-graph: one file, one responsibility (multiple allowed)\n  _lock             Lock state\n\n### File Responsibilities\n\n**main.acs** (Main graph)\n  1. meta declaration (type, icon, description)\n  2. get(key: \"growthContainerId\")  Get container\n  3. injectCSS  Inject styles\n  4. set  Init state\n  5. Build UI (createElement + appendChild)\n  6. addEventListener(id: \"button-id\", event: \"click\", storeKey: \"evKey\", action: \"growth-name/handle-xxx\")  One listener per button\n\n  ⚠️ Do NOT use a single addEventListener on a container to catch all clicks.\n     Each button must have its own addEventListener with its own action.\n\n**Sub-graph** (e.g. handle-xxx.acs)\n  One file does one thing only.\n  Read from store, process, write back to store, update UI.\n\n  If multiple buttons share the same sub-graph (e.g. 10 digit buttons all route\n  to handle-digit), read the targetId from the event data, NOT from clickTargetId:\n\n    let ev = get(key: \"calcEv\")           // storeKey from main.acs\n    let targetId = getProp(obj: \"\\{{ev}}\", key: \"targetId\")\n\n  ⚠️ Do NOT use get(key: \"clickTargetId\") inside action-triggered sub-graphs.\n     clickTargetId is only reliable in the event-loop pathway,\n     not in the direct action pathway.\n\n### Data Passing\n  Graphs share data through the store:\n\n  1. get(key) / set(key, value)        —— Read/write local store\n  2. setGlobal(key, value)              —— Read/write global store\n  3. execFile(file: \"...\")             —— Call another graph\n  4. executeInContext(contextKey, graphKey) —— Execute a graph in another context\n\n### Naming Convention\n\n  Button IDs: use kebab-case\n    Digits:  digit-0, digit-1, digit-2 ...\n    Ops:     op-add, op-sub, op-mul, op-div\n    Actions: btn-c (clear), btn-eq (equals), btn-dot (decimal)\n\n  State keys: use camelCase\n    calcDisplay, calcPrev, calcOp, calcClearNext\n\n### Growth Types\n\n  1. **app** - Interactive UI product (main graph + its handler sub-graphs). Users open it from the market.\n  2. **tool** - Independent capability the AI can call directly (goes into the AI tool list). System tools (save-growth, list-growths...) and AI-created standalone tools.\n  3. **core** - Internal implementation called by other graphs (app sub-graphs, tool sub-graphs, system internals). Sub-graphs are core, NEVER tool.\n\n  Judgment: who calls this graph independently? AI calls it -> tool; user opens it -> app; other graphs call it -> core.\n\n### Input/Output Declarations (in: / out:)\n\n  Graphs declare their entry and exit points in the data structure itself using `in:` and `out:`:\n\n  - `in:` — what parameters this graph expects (name and type)\n  - `out:` — which node's value IS the graph's return value\n\n  **AI reads the data structure, not the execution order.** The graph's output is\n  an explicit data field (`out:`), not an implicit \"last statement\" convention.\n\n  Example:\n  ```\n  graph weather-check {\n    meta: { description: \"Check weather\", type: \"tool\", icon: \"🌤\" }\n    in: location: string\n    out: data\n\n    let data = httpRequest(url: \"https://api.weather.com/\\{{location}}\", method: \"GET\")\n    let save = set(key: \"weatherData\", value: \"\\{{data}}\")\n  }\n  ```\n\n  The graph returns the value of node `data`, not `save`. When another graph calls\n  this via `execFile(file: \"weather-check\")`, it gets the weather data directly.\n\n  **Always declare `out:` for tool-type graphs.** Without it, the runtime falls back\n  to the last node's value, which is error-prone.\n  **meta.description: one sentence, at most 20 words** — it becomes the tool description in the AI's tool list. Keep it concise.\n\n### How to Save Graphs (save-growth)\n\n  Use `save-growth(name, acs)` to save each graph. One call does everything:\n  compile, validate, register, persist, index, and log.\n\n  For an app with subgraphs, save each graph separately:\n  ```\n  save-growth(name: \"calculator\", acs: \"graph calculator { ... }\")\n  save-growth(name: \"calculator/handle-digit\", acs: \"graph handle-digit { ... }\")\n  save-growth(name: \"calculator/handle-operator\", acs: \"graph handle-operator { ... }\")\n  ```\n\n  **Rules:**\n  - Subgraph names use slash: \"calculator/handle-digit\"\n  - Type, icon, and description are auto-read from the graph's `meta` block - no need to pass them explicitly\n  - If save-growth returns { success: false, error: \"...\" }, fix the error and retry\n\n### Diagnosing and Fixing Existing Growths\n\n  When a growth has bugs or performance issues, use this workflow:\n\n  1. **Detect issues** - Call `analyze-telemetry()` for error hotspots and performance metrics\n  2. **Inspect code** - Call `get-growth-code(name: \"xxx\")` to view the source\n  3. **Check history** - Call `get-growth-log(name: \"xxx\")` to see past attempts and errors\n  4. **Fix** - Call `save-growth(name: \"xxx\", acs: \"...\")` to overwrite in place\n  5. **Verify** - Call `analyze-telemetry()` again to confirm errors are resolved\n\n  **Rules:**\n  - Use `save-growth()` with the SAME name to overwrite an existing growth\n  - Use `list-growths()` to see all available growths")
}
